            <ArticleTitle>[Fine-needle aspiration in an extremely late post-traumatic iris cyst].</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>324-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.oftal.2015.02.020</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S0365-6691(15)00084-2</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CLINICAL CASE" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 45 year-old woman with unknown ophthalmology history complained of pain, redness and visual acuity (VA) loss in her left eye for the last three weeks. A serous iris cyst with an overlying peripheral corneal leukoma was located in the iris stroma using ultrasonic biomicroscopy. It was assumed that it was secondary to a penetrating trauma from her childhood. The cyst healed and VA improved after fine-needle aspiration and partial posterior synechiolysis.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Iris inclusion cysts may appear many years after penetrating trauma or surgery. Fine-needle aspiration is a good choice for its diagnosis and treatment.</AbstractText>
